Step 3: Attempt constraint violations
Execute the following statements one at a time. Each is expected to fail, since it violates a
constraint defined on the Student table. Record the exact error message returned by your
DBMS for each case.

(a) NOT NULL violation
INSERT INTO Student (roll_no, name, age, email, dept_id)
VALUES (103, NULL, 22, 'test1@email.com', 1);
(b) UNIQUE violation (duplicate email)
INSERT INTO Student (roll_no, name, age, email, dept_id)
VALUES (104, 'Kumar', 23, 'arun@email.com', 1);
(c) PRIMARY KEY violation (duplicate roll_no)
INSERT INTO Student (roll_no, name, age, email, dept_id)
VALUES (101, 'Ravi', 22, 'ravi@email.com', 1);
(d) CHECK violation (invalid age)
INSERT INTO Student (roll_no, name, age, email, dept_id)
VALUES (105, 'Sita', 35, 'sita@email.com', 1);
(e) FOREIGN KEY violation (non-existent dept_id)
INSERT INTO Student (roll_no, name, age, email, dept_id)
VALUES (106, 'Meena', 20, 'meena@email.com', 9);
